  h2('21.1  Intelligence Brief Research Prompt'),
  callout('Intelligence Brief Prompt', 'You are a market intelligence analyst for [CLIENT NAME], a [VERTICAL] in [GEOGRAPHY] with [X] years in business serving clients at [PRICE POINT].\n\nSearch for and analyze:\n1. Any notable moves by their top 3 competitors in the past 30 days: [COMPETITOR 1], [COMPETITOR 2], [COMPETITOR 3]. Look for new content, new services, awards, reviews, hires, or market positioning changes.\n2. Any market trends in the [VERTICAL] industry in [GEOGRAPHY/REGION] in the past 30 days that a sophisticated practitioner would find relevant.\n3. Any content gaps — topics that buyers in this market are actively searching for that no competitor is currently addressing well.\n\nOutput: A structured Intelligence Brief with three sections: Market Signal, Competitor Watch, and The Opportunity. Each section: 100–150 words. Specific, not generic. Every claim should be traceable to a real observation.', C.navy),
  h2('21.2  Authority Asset — The Guide Prompt'),
  callout('Guide Drafting Prompt', 'You are a senior content strategist writing for [CLIENT NAME], a [VERTICAL] in [GEOGRAPHY] founded in [YEAR] with [X years] of experience. Your voice is authoritative but accessible — the voice of a trusted expert explaining complex decisions to an intelligent buyer who is not an expert.\n\nWrite a comprehensive buyer\'s guide titled: "[TITLE]"\n\nTarget reader: [BUYER PROFILE — e.g., "A family relocating from a major metro area planning their first custom home in the Texas Hill Country, budget $1.2–2M, timeline 18 months"]\n\nThe guide must cover:\n[LIST 6–8 SPECIFIC SECTIONS FROM CONTENT BRIEF]\n\nTone: Expert. Direct. No jargon without explanation. No marketing language. This reads like it was written by the most experienced practitioner in the field who also happens to be an excellent teacher.\n\nLength: 1,800–2,500 words.\n\nDo not include any generic statements that could apply to any business in this category. Every paragraph should be specific enough that a competitor could not publish it without modification.', C.navy),
  h2('21.3  Case Study Prompt'),
  callout('Case Study Prompt', 'You are writing a client case study for [CLIENT NAME] about the following project:\n\nClient profile: [AGE/SITUATION/LOCATION — no full name unless permitted]\nProject type: [DESCRIPTION]\nKey challenge: [WHAT MADE THIS PROJECT COMPLEX OR SIGNIFICANT]\nApproach: [HOW THE CLIENT HANDLED IT]\nOutcome: [MEASURABLE OR EXPERIENTIAL RESULT]\nClient quote: "[VERBATIM QUOTE IF AVAILABLE, OR PARAPHRASED SENTIMENT]"\n\nWrite a 500–700 word case study in narrative form — not a bulleted list. Open with a scene or moment that puts the reader in the client\'s shoes. Build through the challenge and the process. End with the outcome and a reflection on what it means.\n\nThis case study should make a prospective buyer in a similar situation think: "That is exactly my situation. I want this provider."', C.navy),
  h2('21.4  Email Newsletter Prompt'),
  callout('Email Newsletter Prompt', 'Write a monthly email newsletter for [CLIENT NAME] to send to their existing network of past clients, referral partners, and professional contacts.\n\nThis month\'s topic / insight: [TOPIC FROM CONTENT BRIEF OR INTELLIGENCE BRIEF]\n\nTone: Practitioner to peer. Personal. One insight, explained clearly, with a direct application. This is not a marketing email. It is a practitioner\'s note — the kind of email a respected expert sends to their network when they have learned something worth sharing.\n\nStructure:\n- Subject line (3 options, no clickbait)\n- Opening (1–2 sentences — a scene, a question, or an observation)\n- The insight (150–200 words)\n- The application (50–75 words — what should the reader do or think differently)\n- The close (2–3 sentences — warm, no hard call to action)\n\nThe reader should finish this email thinking more highly of [CLIENT NAME] than they did before they opened it.', C.navy),
  h2('21.5  Community Landing Page Prompt'),
  callout('Community / Specialty Page Prompt', 'Write a community-specific service page for [CLIENT NAME]\'s website targeting [COMMUNITY/AREA NAME] in [GEOGRAPHY].\n\nTarget search query: "[SPECIFIC QUERY — e.g., \'custom home builder Cordillera Ranch\']"\n\nInclude:\n1. Opening paragraph establishing the client\'s specific experience in this community (use real details: years, named projects if permitted, community knowledge)\n2. Why building/working in this community requires specific expertise (terrain, HOA, architectural requirements, access, etc.)\n3. What the client offers specifically for buyers in this community\n4. Social proof — one or two testimonials from clients in this community (or nearby if none specific)\n5. A direct call to action for prospects in this community\n\nLength: 500–700 words. SEO-optimized but human-first — this page should be as useful to a buyer reading it as it is to a search engine indexing it.', C.navy),
  h2('21.6  Video Script Prompt'),
  callout('Video Script Prompt', 'Write a video script for [CLIENT NAME], the founder/owner of [BUSINESS], for a [4–6 minute] video titled: "[TITLE]"\n\nFormat: The founder speaks directly to camera. No teleprompter feel — this should sound like a practiced professional having a direct conversation with a buyer who is in the research phase.\n\nTopic: [SPECIFIC TOPIC FROM CONTENT BRIEF]\n\nStructure:\n- Opening hook (10–15 seconds) — a question, a counterintuitive fact, or a problem statement that the viewer immediately recognizes\n- The insight (2–3 minutes) — the core teaching, explained clearly, with a specific example or story\n- The common mistake (45–60 seconds) — what most buyers or competitors get wrong about this topic\n- The close (30 seconds) — a direct invitation to take the next step, no pressure\n\nWrite it to be spoken, not read. Short sentences. Conversational cadence. The viewer should feel like the most knowledgeable person in the room just sat down across from them.', C.navy),

